{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "וכתבתם על מזוזות ביתך", :translit "u-khtavtem al mezuzot beitekha", :gloss "and you shall write them on the doorposts of your house"} {:he "למען ירבו", :translit "lema'an yirbu", :gloss "that [your days] may be multiplied"} {:he "אם שמור תשמרון", :translit "im shomor tishmerun", :gloss "if you shall diligently keep"} {:he "על שם ה' שומרך", :translit "al shem Hashem shomrekha", :gloss "on the name of the LORD your guardian"} {:he "ה' צלך על יד ימינך", :translit "Hashem tzelkha al yad yaminekha", :gloss "the LORD your shade upon your right hand"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "semikhut ha-parashiyot: juxtaposition of adjacent verses to derive that mezuzot protect from the destroyer"} {:kind :other, :note "semikhut ha-parashiyot: juxtaposition to derive divine guarding through mezuzah from Psalm 121:5"}], :frame {:text "The principle of semikhut ha-parashiyot (juxtaposition of verses) derives halakhic or aggadic teachings from the placement of adjacent passages. The protective power of mezuzot is expressed in tractate Menahot 43b, where R. Hanina wore a mezuzah and was protected. Psalm 121:5 identifies God as 'your shade upon your right hand' (ה' צלך על יד ימינך), the verse Ba'al HaTurim cites as the tradition underlying the juxtaposition. The phrase 'the destroyer does not enter' resonates with the Passover protection of Exodus 12:23, where the destroyer (המשחית) is barred from houses bearing the blood mark.", :sources ["Menahot 43b" "Psalm 121:5" "Exodus 12:23"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "semikhut ha-parashiyot: juxtaposition of adjacent verses to derive that mezuzot protect from the destroyer", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "semikhut ha-parashiyot: juxtaposition to derive divine guarding through mezuzah from Psalm 121:5", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:35:55.620250965Z", :model "glm-5.2"}